      "id": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50577265",
      "title": "Language Models Can Rewrite Their Own Weights",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21885",
      "commentsUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50577265",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T16:29:06Z",
      "score": 347,
      "comments": 112,
      "snippet": "[2611.21885] Language Models Can Rewrite Their Own Weights Skip to main content Search Submit Donate Log in Search arXiv Press Enter to search &middot; Advanced search --> Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence arXiv:2611.21885 (cs) [Submitted on 16 Nov 2026 ( v1 ), last revised 18 Nov 2026 (this version, v2)] Title: Language Models Can Rewrite Their Own Weights Authors: Jiajun Fan , Neil He , Changyi Yang , Andy Mao , Xi Zhu , Chaoran Cheng , Kaiqu Liang , Shuaike Shen , Zetian Li , Ruihan Guo , Ge Liu View a PDF of the paper titled Language Models Can Rewrite Their Own Weights, by Jiajun Fan and 10 other authors View PDF HTML (experimental) Abstract: A language model is trained once and frozen. Making it better takes another training run, or a prompt, a harness or extra computation at inference that is gone once the task ends. We show a third way: to our knowledge for the first time, a language model rewrites its own weights by its own reasoning and becomes better, with no gradients and no training. We call this self-rewriting: the model is its own optimizer. In earlier work, a frontier model edited a much smaller one; here an open 27B model, given its weights, its graded answers and the ability to run itself, diagnoses its own failure and rewrites the weights behind it. It surpasses itself: its own edit makes it 6.6 points more accurate on problems it never saw. It keeps getting better: it tests its edits on practice problems and submits only what works, each edit improving on the last, and over eight consecutive sessions its accuracy only rises. It repairs a damaged copy of itself, like a broken robot that fixes itself without being rebuilt: the copy solves almost nothing, yet with no retraining and without ever reading the original weights, the model brings it back to over 80% of the original accuracy on image tasks it never saw. And the ability emerges with scale: in one model family, the smaller models make no edit or break themselves, and only the largest improves and repairs itself. Unlike test-time scaling, self-rewriting writes its gain into the weights, at no cost per answer; unlike training, it runs only forward passes, as serving does. The wall between training and inference is a limit of our tools, not a property of intelligence. We believe self-rewriting is a necessary step toward recursive self-improvement: a model that, like a human, does not separate training from inference could keep upgrading itself for life, with no larger model, no harness or prompt engineering, and no pre- or post-training. Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) Cite as: arXiv:2611.21885 [cs.AI] (or arXiv:2611.21885v2 [cs.AI] for this version) https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.21885 Focus to learn more arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite Submission history From: Jiajun Fan [ view email ] [v1] Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:55:08 UTC (6,363 KB) [v2] Wed, 18 Nov 2026 08:57:59 UTC (6,379 KB) Full-text links: Access Paper: View a PDF of the paper titled Language Model",
      "oneLineKo": "언어모델이 추론만으로 자신의 가중치를 스스로 수정",
      "summaryKo": "이 논문은 추가 학습이나 그래디언트 없이, 언어모델이 자신의 추론만으로 자기 가중치를 직접 수정(self-rewriting)하는 방법을 제시한다. 27B 오픈 모델이 자신의 오답을 진단하고 가중치를 수정해 미지의 문제에서 정확도가 6.6포인트 향상되었으며, 8번의 연속 세션 동안 성능이 계속 개선되었다. 또한 손상된 복제본을 원본 가중치 참조 없이 복구해 원래 정확도의 80% 이상을 회복했고, 이 능력은 모델 크기가 커질수록만 나타났다. 저자들은 이를 학습과 추론의 경계를 허무는 재귀적 자기개선의 필수 단계로 제시한다.",
      "tags": [
        "llm",
        "self-improvement",
        "model-weights"
      ]
    },
