      "id": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50579331",
      "title": "We promoted developers into architects without informing them",
      "url": "https://manidoraisamy.com/developer-to-architect.html",
      "commentsUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50579331",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T18:17:51Z",
      "score": 322,
      "comments": 188,
      "snippet": "We promoted developers into architects without informing them Developer forever Home Posts About Contact Read my posts Read my posts All posts We promoted developers into architects without informing them Mani Doraisamy 8 hours ago 3 1 Yesterday, I was debugging a performance problem with our Google Forms addon. It turned out my colleague had vibe coded the logging part, so that he could monitor the new installs every week. Monitoring new customers is the most important part of running a business, so he is absolutely right in doing that. Except, he was logging the user’s email and Google has severely restricted how much detail you can get about the user since last December. So, AI had written a series of try/catch statements: First, try to get the email if the user gave consent to share it during installation. If it fails, it would try to fetch his user ID. If that fails, it would log an error saying the user has installed, but we don’t know his email or user ID. All of these are one line of code, but internally each of these makes multiple API calls to Google servers. Since logging was the first line to execute after installation, the welcome screen took 15 seconds to load. What is the right code for this? If you are a senior developer or architect who understands the number of API calls behind each method, you would have: Logged just the timestamp of the install instead of trying to fetch the email or user ID, and told the marketing team they won’t be able to send the follow-up email based on installation alone. Or, if marketing wants the user’s email on install, you would have shown the welcome screen first and logged the email or user ID asynchronously from the browser, while the user is looking at the welcome screen. But calling your server from the addon’s welcome screen might require your server to bypass security and log an unauthenticated payload. In other words, your job as an architect is to push back on requirements that can slow down the app. Or, find a way to work around the performance and security issues by making it async or accepting a reasonable hack. Why AI won’t push back AI is great at writing code. But it doesn’t know if you are launching the app or have millions of users, which it needs to make the right architecture call to log a timestamp vs email. It is also sycophantic and tries to execute your requirements exactly without pushing back, which means you end up with a slow app that takes 15 seconds to load a static welcome screen. Essentially, if you generate code by specifying your requirements instead of specifying your architecture, AI will decide the architecture on your behalf. It is so paranoid about security that it will never write code to accept an unauthenticated payload. Even if you write it yourself, it will rewrite it into an authenticated payload and screw up performance or force users to sign in again on your app. Your new job is to architect AI coding tools are tuned to implement your functional requireme",
      "oneLineKo": "AI 코딩이 개발자를 암묵적으로 아키텍트로 승격시켰다",
      "summaryKo": "저자는 동료가 AI로 로깅 코드를 작성했다가 다수의 API 호출로 인해 웰컴 화면 로딩이 15초나 걸리는 성능 문제를 겪은 사례를 소개한다. AI는 요구사항을 그대로 구현할 뿐 아키텍처적 판단(언제 타임스탬프만 로깅할지, 언제 비동기 처리할지 등)을 하지 않으며, 보안에 대해서도 과도하게 보수적이라 비인증 페이로드를 거부해 성능을 해친다. 따라서 개발자는 이제 요구사항뿐 아니라 아키텍처 자체를 명시해야 하는 아키텍트 역할을 암묵적으로 맡게 되었다고 주장한다.",
      "tags": [
        "ai-coding",
        "software-architecture",
        "performance"
      ]
    },
